The solution is the general concurrent updating procedure:
When a player's game ends, read the file from the server with update
lock, so nobody else can access it while it is locked, update it with
the new entry, then release the lock.
There is no reason to use stale copies of the file during the process.
When the file is read with an update lock, it is not stale.

Actually, you could just send the new score entry to the server, let the
server decide whether or not the entry causes an update to the score
list, then return the (updated or unchanged) record file to the client.
That way it doesn't matter whether the client's file was up-to-date or
not before, it will be after the fresh file is returned from the server.

